iscussing their adverse effects  followed by beneficial effects  of course they are two sides of the same  coin i'm starting with the adverse  effects  because that's the most important part  to toxicology  i'll also speak briefly about  pharmacokinetic concerns  for interpreting blood and other body  fluid concentrations  and some risk factors for toxicity  so to get started with the good the bad  and the ugly effects  let's talk about the mechanism of action  of this class of drugs  it begins with quinine a substance that  was  extracted from sinchona bark  in peru and it was used in india and sri  lanka by the british empire as a tonic  to prevent malaria it was a very  economical treatment  it's also the bitter agent that's added  to tonic water  there are two other molecules used as  drugs based on this  same four amino quinoline structure as  quinine  there are chloroquine which came first  and hydroxychloroquine  adding a hydroxy group to the  chloroquine molecule  i called this slide the law of  unintended consequences  these drugs have diverse mechanisms of  action  they act on many different sites inside  of the cell and the body  and because of that they can have a  variety of different effects  both good and bad so first of all  there's the structural similarity to the  prostaglandins and cytokines  which allow these molecules to inhibit  the enzymes in the inflammatory cascade  they're also charged molecules and this  feature allows them to accumulate in  lysosomes  which is particularly important to their  treatment of malaria  they also inhibit endosomes that are  involved in transport in the cell  and this feature is also the reason that  they bind to melanin which will become  important when we talk about  retinopathy as a side effect  a third mechanism of action is their  ability to block the pore of potassium  channels  and the picture at the bottom of the  slide shows the hydroxychloroquine  molecule docked inside  of an inwardly rectifying potassium  channel so it  effectively plugs that hole and prevents  potassium from passing through  finally hydroxychloroquine and  chloroquine  both act as zinc ionophores so they can  shepherd zinc molecules within the cell  zinc is an important component of rna  replication  and the ionophore feature allows these  molecules to inhibit  rna replication so of course this would  slow the  division replication of the virus  this is a summary of the different  mechanisms of action  and how they are both visible for  adverse effects and beneficial effects  so first being the synthesis if you  inhibit prostaglandin synthesis  you can cause immunosuppression which  might not be a good thing  if you system emotions  however this also has a beneficial  effect anti-inflammatory  and this is the reason inhibiting  prostaglandin synthesis  is inhibiting thromboxane  which is primarily involved in clotting  so hydroxychloroquine has anticoagulant  functions  the second mechanism the increased ph of  lysosomes  contributes to the adverse effect of  retinopathy  in this case it inhibits transporters in  the retinal pigment epithelium  and it accumulates in the eye and can  cause  death of those cells however  it's also a beneficial effect because  this is what gives it its ability to  fight malaria  and also because it stops transport  across cell membranes  hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine have  both been investigated  to be combined with chemotherapeutics  because they help to  reduce resistance that cancer cells have  by ejecting  the chemotherapeutics from inside the  cell  the third mechanism of action blocking  potassium channels  seems to contribute to the cardiac  toxicity  of these drugs including contributing to  hypokalemia  as well as arrhythmia finally  the zinc ionophore disrupts right so the  beneficial effect of that is that  these drugs can act as antivirals  however  cause edge to rapidly tissue  need similar zinc finger domains in  order to replicate  dna and that can be  effects seen primarily in the gi tract  gastrointestinal tract  as well as bone marrow  i'm going to talk in detail about each  of the adverse effects of  hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine  first cardiovascular effects  the main feature of hydroxychloroquine  and chloroquine is that they can  increase the qt interval this is thought  to be mediated by the hypokalemia that  they cause  the longer qt interval  can contribute to arrhythmias and  increase risk of a rhythmic mortality  second blood dyscrasias  this is unknown adverse  hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine as  well  because of the zinc ionophore that  impairs rna and dna replication  and can cause bone marrow failure  some different blood dyscrasias reported  after using these compounds include a  plastic anemia  a granulocytosis leukopenia  and thrombocytopenia it's particularly  important to note that individuals who  already have a genetic disposition  towards a plastic anemia  with glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase  deficiency  are contraindicated for using  hydrochloroquine and chloroquine  first effect is retinopathy  more effects have also been seen with  hearing loss and bleaching of hair  because hydroxychloroquine and  chloroquine bind to melanin  they're likely to interact and sequester  with melanin-containing tissues  including the choroid of the eye  they cause damage to these cells usually  around the macula  which can cause irreversible blindness  they induce vacuole formation through  their lysosomal  eh altering ability and that results in  cell lysis and retinal death  for this reason a maximum dosage of five  milligrams per kilogram daily  has been recommended for  hydroxychloroquine  next are adverse effects in the central  nervous system  these drugs have been shown to lower the  seizure threshold  the mechanism is not well characterized  however it does appear to be related to  inhibition of gabaergic signals and  gabaergic is the primary  depressant in the central nervous system  so inhibition of gabaergic  neurotransmission would tend to be  excitatory  thus contributing to the risk of  seizures  other additional adverse effects in the  central nervous system can include  dizziness taxia muscular weakness and  tremors  also psychiatric adverse effects include  nervousness  irritability and psychosis mares  and even suicidal behavior  among the adverse effects of  hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine  are multiple drugs  so there are interactions  different drugs used to treat heart  disease  important to note amiodarone  an increased risk of arrhythmia if  hydroxychloroquine is combined with  amiodarone  as well as with any other drug that  could lengthen the qt interval  while increased plasma cyclosporine  levels were reported  when hydrochlor hydroxychloroquine was  co-administered  the tendency for hydroxychloroquine and  chloroquine to  cause hypoglycemia could mean a dose  adjustment is warranted  for insulin or other hypoglycemic drugs  and finally there are different effects  of acids on chloroquine concentrations  so they might not be able to be taken  together  here are some details about hypoglycemia  that's caused by  hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine it's  rarely severe but it has been reported  even in non-diabetic patients  ethanol consumption is a risk factor for  this type of hypoglycemia  the drugs inhibit insulin metabolism  which causes increased concentrations of  insulin in the blood  and therefore promoted glucose transport  into the cells  overall the blood concentration of  glucose is depleted  and the brain experiences this as  hypoglycemia  which can cause dizziness and weakness  there's also a beneficial effect though  because  diabetic patients will actually have  improved glycemic control  while they're taking these drugs  there are four main risk factors for  toxicity  the first are individuals with glucose  6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency  of hemolytic anemia the second are  patients with liver disease  caution should be used in patients with  hepatic disease alcoholism  or hepatotoxic drugs and this is  primarily due to combined  toxic effects on the liver  such as cardiomyopathy epilepsy and  diabetes  we'll need careful consideration when  using hydroxyl  and chloroquine and there are some age  groups that are particularly vulnerable  to these drugs  for example chloroquine has been shown  to cause birth defects  in animals infants and children  are noted to be extremely sensitive to  the toxic effects of all four amino acid  alums  and geriatric patients may have impaired  excretion due to reduced renal function  now the other side of the coin so any  substance that produces  active effects whether they be adverse  or beneficial  can also prove produce the opposite side  of the coin  the beneficial effects also have a  variety of  mechanisms that they use targeting  lysosomes being the original  and older mechanism of action  four amino quinolones accumulate in  lysosomes becoming charged in the acidic  environment  and this prevents them from diffusing  out so they accumulate in that lysosome  change the ph of it and can cause  swelling or malfunction of the organ  this makes them toxic to plasmodium  parasites  which is a great benefit for treating  malaria  they also inhibit notable enzyme  function including the mhc class ii  antigen presentation on monocytes  because they selectively inhibit  presentation of  self antigens they're very useful for  the treatment of autoimmune  in particular systemic lupus  erythematosus  cysms is also a mechanism of action  related to antiviral properties  of hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine  because they increase the ph of  lysosomes they inhibit viral entry  into the cell viruses need to move  from the early endosomes abbreviated eea  1  until late endosomal lysosomal protein  abbreviated lamp 1  in order to be released into the cell  and this step is ph dependent  i have a graph from a recent publication  showing  untreated cells chloroquine treated  cells  and hydroxychloroquine treated cells and  where  viral proteins were localized within the  endosomes  you can see that chloroquine and  hydroxychloroquine  held viral proteins inside of early  endosomes and prevented them from  reaching  late endosomes this would also help to  prevent their infection of cells  some additional actions of  hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine  they enhance zinc uptake into cells  which helps to interfere with rna  polymerase  they can also inhibit  glycosyltransferase enzymes  and it's glycosylated ace2 receptors  that are the anti  site for severe acute respiratory  coronaviruses  and finally they inhibit the clotting  cascade  and platelet aggregation which is  beneficial in patients with covid19  because they can have hyper  hypercoagulation  as a late manifestation of the disease  now some words about pharmacokinetics i  have each  drug on its own slide hydroxychloroquine  is first  for absorption it's well absorbed with  70 to 80 percent bioavailability  following oral dosing it's  distributed very widely throughout the  body with a large volume of distribution  of 47  000 liters this is because it can bind  to cellular components of  blood including platelets and can also  concentrate in tissues  such as the liver lungs and other organs  it is metabolized primarily by sip 2d6  to the active metabolite  des-ethyl hydroxychloroquine and has a  very long elimination half-life  537 hours in blood which is the same as  22 days and the active metabolite is an  even longer half-life of 40 to 50 days  these compounds can both remain  detectable in urine for months after  treatment is stopped  chloroquine has similar pharmacokinetics  to hydroxychloroquine  89 bioavailability  plasma protein binding of 60  and even larger volume of distribution  at 65 000 liters  it's metabolized also by sip enzymes but  different ones in this case  3a45 and 2c8  and has two active metabolites does  ethyl chloroquine  and bi-diethyl chloroquine  the half-life of elimination increases  with dose and two examples are  following 250 milligram dose three hours  but following a thousand milligram dose  300 hours  or 13 days again detectable in urine for  months  because of these properties the large  volume of distribution in particular  pre-analytical factors are a concern in  the analysis of hydroxychloroquine  and chloroquine keep in mind that the  mechanism of these drugs includes being  sequestered in red blood cells  chloroquine can also absorb to glass so  it should be collected and stored in  plastic  and because of this tendency to  sequester in red blood cells  the blood concentrations tend to be  higher than those in serum  you can also get irregular serum  recovery depending on how long  you clot the sample and what speed you  centrifuge you're at  so it can be more straightforward to  interpret blood reference ranges  if they're not collected by the same  study parameters  as the reference range  the blood to plasma ratio is also high  at five to ten  postmortem blood concentrations in  non-toxic cases  overlap the concentrations reported in  fatal cases that's not uncommon for  drugs with a large volume of  distribution  and it makes it difficult to use a final  postmortem concentration  to interpret whether a dose was toxic or  not  it may be due to postmortem  redistribution of these compounds  so just note to interpret postmortem  concentrations with caution  for specimen handling these compounds  are both  remarkably stable at room temperature in  a variety of fluids  it's also possible to analyze  chloroquine as a dried blood spot  and that can be kept at room temperature  for 1.2 years with desiccant  and maintain a similar concentration as  when it was originally collected  analytical techniques are reported in  a great variety and abundance since  these drugs have been around for  many decades lcms  has two references here and you can see  the measured range usually starts close  to 10 or 20 nanograms per milliliter  most of these methods are in whole blood  and one of them in capillary blood  for sampling smaller volumes this is  again because it's easier to interpret  concentrations in the whole blood matrix  since you don't have to account for the  variability of recovery following  clotting  there's also a method called turbulent  flow  lcms that's used for  whole blood analysis and two different  screening techniques  gcms a mainstay of forensic toxicology  and lc tof time of flight mass  spectrometry  chloroquine being older and perhaps not  as commonly encountered  today by forensic toxicologists does  have  some publications even in recent  literature  an lcms method for analyzing whole blood  plasma and dried blood spots  a similar lower limit of quantitation to  what we saw for hydroxychloroquine  about 10 or perhaps slightly lower than  that nanograms per ml in blood  spectrofluorometry was also used in the  1970s  gcnpd can be used as a technique for  quantitation  and lc time of flight mass spectrometry  is also effective at screening for  chloroquine  if you happen to identify these  compounds through screening  and are able to get a concentration it  can be  interpreted with caution against  published reference ranges  it might not be likely to detect them  with general unknown panels however so  you might need to look at case history  to determine which samples should be  sent for quantitative analysis  the concentrations for therapeutic use  of these compounds are both similar  200 nanogram per ml is the average  concentration in responding  rheumatoid arthritis to patients  chloroquine  and 200 is the cutoff for non-adherence  in lupus patients  both of them have a therapeutic range  around 400 to 500 nanogram per ml  but note that the therapeutic ranges for  chloroquine are being reported in serum  in this case  so they could be very different in blood  hydroxychloroquine concentrations are  reported in blood  and high therapeutic concentrations  tend to be about two to three times  higher  than those average concentrations with  therapeutic use  in non-fatal overdose hydroxychloroquine  concentrations as high as 26 000  nanogram per ml in serum have been  reported  that case did require resuscitation and  involved a 36-hour coma post-mortem  non-toxic  and overdose ranges were also reported  and you can see the overlap there where  non-toxic concentrations can be as high  as 39 000 nanograms per ml in blood  while post-mortem overdose can be as low  as 20 000.  similar thing is seen for chloroquine  with post-mortem non-toxic as high as 14  000 nanogram per ml  and overdose starting as low as 3 000.  it was actually recommended by one  publication to use  liver for interpreting chloroquine  postmortem  150 mg per kilogram was established as a  threshold for overdose  in summary hydroxychloroquine and  chloroquine have diverse mechanisms  and may cause a spectrum of adverse  effects these can include  cardiac arrhythmia retinopathy  lowered seizure threshold and blood  dyscrasias  medical conditions as well and drug  interactions increase the risk of  adverse effects  these can include glucose 6-phosphate  deficiency drugs that increase the qt  interval such as digoxin  drug interactions infants and children  are also known to be more sensitive to  toxic effects  and finally blood or plasma should be  collected  in plastic tubes and compared to a  matching reference range  or normalized with the blood to plasma  ratio in mind  and consider liver concentration for  postmortem interpretation  in my last slide i have included several  resources for further information  there are many different drug  interaction checkers on  the internet some examples are here  the benefit of the interaction checker  is that you can enter  multiple drugs that are found in a case  and see if they interact with each other  to cause side effects  for example entering azithromycin with  hydroxychloroquine  will give you an indication of a  moderate interaction  because both drugs can extend the qt  interval  making them arrhythmic  some government resources include daily  med  which is a great way to search for full  prescribing information  using either the brand or generic name  of a drug  the drug information portal is very  useful  if you're uncertain of the spelling of a  drug so it might have been transcribed  incorrectly by somebody at a crime scene  and you can put in a misspelled drug  name and  the drug information portal will give  you a list of the best matches  it also links to daily med  the clinicaltrials.gov page includes  international trials as well as u.s  trials  at the moment there are 79 active  studies for hydroxychloroquine  including the keyword treatment of covid  and finally fda that is a  great site to go to for history of drug  approvals and current status  you can also subscribe there to updates  for recalls and safety alerts  that's the end of my presentation i  thank you for your time and i hope you  enjoy  your education experience at lab roots

