oquine  and bi-diethyl chloroquine  the half-life of elimination increases  with dose and two examples are  following 250 milligram dose three hours  but following a thousand milligram dose  300 hours  or 13 days again detectable in urine for  months  because of these properties the large  volume of distribution in particular  pre-analytical factors are a concern in  the analysis of hydroxychloroquine  and chloroquine keep in mind that the  mechanism of these drugs includes being  sequestered in red blood cells  chloroquine can also absorb to glass so  it should be collected and stored in  plastic  and because of this tendency to  sequester in red blood cells  the blood concentrations tend to be  higher than those in serum  you can also get irregular serum  recovery depending on how long  you clot the sample and what speed you  centrifuge you're at  so it can be more straightforward to  interpret blood reference ranges  if they're not collected by the same  study parameters  as the reference range  the blood to plasma ratio is also high  at five to ten  postmortem blood concentrations in  non-toxic cases  overlap the concentrations reported in  fatal cases that's not uncommon for  drugs with a large volume of  distribution  and it makes it difficult to use a final  postmortem concentration  to interpret whether a dose was toxic or  not  it may be due to postmortem  redistribution of these compounds  so just note to interpret postmortem  concentrations with caution  for specimen handling these compounds  are both  remarkably stable at room temperature in  a variety of fluids  it's also possible to analyze  chloroquine as a dried blood spot  and that can be kept at room temperature  for 1.2 years with desiccant  and maintain a similar concentration as  when it was originally collected  analytical techniques are reported in  a great variety and abundance since  these drugs have been around for  many decades lcms  has two references here and you can see  the measured range usually starts close  to 10 or 20 nanograms per milliliter  most of these methods are in whole blood  and one of them in capillary blood  for sampling smaller volumes this is  again because it's easier to interpret  concentrations in the whole blood matrix  since you don't have to account for the  variability of recovery following  clotting  there's also a method called turbulent  flow  lcms that's used for  whole blood analysis and two different  screening techniques  gcms a mainstay of forensic toxicology  and lc tof time of flight mass  spectrometry  chloroquine being older and perhaps not  as commonly encountered  today by forensic toxicologists does  have  some publications even in recent  literature  an lcms method for analyzing whole blood  plasma and dried blood spots  a similar lower limit of quantitation to  what we saw for hydroxychloroquine  about 10 or perhaps slightly lower than  that nanograms per ml in blood  spectrofluorometry was also used in the  1970s  gcnpd can be used as a technique for  quantitation  and lc time of flight mass spectrometry  is also effective at screening for  chloroquine  if you happen to identify these  compounds through screening  and are able to get a concentration it  can be  interpreted with caution against  published reference ranges  it might not be likely to detect them  with general unknown panels however so  you might need to look at case history  to determine which samples should be  sent for quantitative analysis  the concentrations for therapeutic use  of these compounds are both similar  200 nanogram per ml is the average  concentration in responding  rheumatoid arthritis to patients  chloroquine  and 200 is the cutoff for non-adherence  in lupus patients  both of them have a therapeutic range  around 400 to 500 nanogram per ml  but note that the therapeutic ranges for  chloroquine are being reported in serum  in this case  so they could be very different in blood  hydroxychloroquine concentrations are  reported in blood  and high therapeutic concentrations  tend to be about two to three times  higher  than those average concentrations with  therapeutic use  in non-fatal overdose hydroxychloroquine  concentrations as high as 26 000  nanogram per ml in serum have been  reported  that case did require resuscitation and  involved a 36-hour coma post-mortem  non-toxic  and overdose ranges were also reported  and you can see the overlap there where  non-toxic concentrations can be as high  as 39 000 nanograms per ml in blood  while post-mortem overdose can be as low  as 20 000.  similar thing is seen for chloroquine  with post-mortem non-toxic as high as 14  000 nanogram per ml  and overdose starting as low as 3 000.  it was actually recommended by one  publication to use  liver for interpreting chloroquine  postmortem  150 mg per kilogram was established as a  threshold for overdose  in summary hydroxychloroquine and  chloroquine have diverse mechanisms  and may cause a spectrum of adverse  effects these can include  cardiac arrhythmia retinopathy  lowered seizure threshold and blood  dyscrasias  medical conditions as well and drug  interactions increase the risk of  adverse effects  these can include glucose 6-phosphate  deficiency drugs that increase the qt  interval such as digoxin  drug interactions infants and children  are also known to be more sensitive to  toxic effects  and finally blood or plasma should be  collected  in plastic tubes and compared to a  matching reference range  or normalized with the blood to plasma  ratio in mind  and consider liver concentration for  postmortem interpretation  in my last slide i have included several  resources for further information  there are many different drug  interaction checkers on  the internet some examples are here  the benefit of the interaction checker  is that you can enter  multiple drugs that are found in a case  and see if they interact with each other  to cause side effects  for example entering azithromycin with  hydroxychloroquine  will give you an indication of a  moderate interaction  because both drugs can extend the qt  interval  making them arrhythmic  some government resources include daily  med  which is a great way to search for full  prescribing information  using either the brand or generic name  of a drug  the drug information portal is very  useful  if you're uncertain of the spelling of a  drug so it might have been transcribed  incorrectly by somebody at a crime scene  and you can put in a misspelled drug  name and  the drug information portal will give  you a list of the best matches  it also links to daily med  the clinicaltrials.gov page includes  international trials as well as u.s  trials  at the moment there are 79 active  studies for hydroxychloroquine  including the keyword treatment of covid  and finally fda that is a  great site to go to for history of drug  approvals and current status  you can also subscribe there to updates  for recalls and safety alerts  that's the end of my presentation i  thank you for your time and i hope you  enjoy  your education experience at lab roots
 

